1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,289
Every design meeting needs a diagram.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,423
So we spend twenty minutes drawing boxes and
arrows…

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,068
and we're left with five minutes to explain
them.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,173
That's why we built Draft Canvas: to flip
that.

5
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:16,503
Sketch a service and a data store.

6
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:18,327
Every shape has a real type.

7
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:20,925
This one is a SQL database.

8
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:23,989
Add a queue, and the arrows label
themselves.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,984
You can draw every box yourself…

10
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:31,149
or hand the pencil to your coding agent.

11
00:00:32,330 --> 00:00:35,999
It reads your code and draws the
architecture, pass by pass.

12
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,835
Keep it plain, or add color when it helps
you explain.

13
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:46,383
And now your agent can sit right beside you,
on the canvas.

14
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:48,311
Meet Nodi!

15
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:54,462
Nodi runs the Claude Code or Codex you
already have, inside the desktop app.

16
00:00:54,570 --> 00:00:56,063
Ask it what could go wrong,

17
00:00:57,130 --> 00:01:01,738
and it answers on the diagram itself,
pinning each point to the part it's about.

18
00:01:03,070 --> 00:01:07,017
Ask for the change, and it draws the
proposal right where it belongs.

19
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:12,302
Compare before and after. Nothing changes
until you accept it.

20
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:18,050
And now, the part that matters most:
explaining it.

21
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:24,097
Start with the whole system, then step into
a service, down to its components.

22
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,603
Then present the flow, one step at a time,

23
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:32,002
so the whole room follows the same request.

24
00:01:34,770 --> 00:01:36,455
Work on it together, live.

25
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:39,847
Every change they make lands on your copy.

26
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,712
Your architecture stays on your device.

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,347
Your agent is the one you already use.

28
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,371
And every decision is yours.

29
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:50,172
Free and open source.

30
00:01:51,230 --> 00:01:56,926
So, the next design meeting: five minutes of
boxes and arrows — and twenty of explaining!

31
00:01:57,530 --> 00:01:59,237
Draw less. Explain more.
